                "reasoning_content": "We need to evaluate each statement given constraints.\n\nFirst define attributes:\n\n- Accepted (A)\n- Passed ethical review (Eth)\n- Has public data (Pub) vs non-public data (NonPub). By definition, NonPub iff not Pub.\n- Undergone replication study (Rep)\n- Pilot study (Pilot)\n- Externally audited (Audit)\n- Multi-center study (Multi)\n- Externally registered (Reg)\n\nGiven facts:\n\n1. Every accepted proposal has passed ethical review. So A -> Eth.\n\n2. Every accepted proposal satisfies exactly one of:\n   (i) it has undergone a replication study (Rep)\n   (ii) it is a Pilot study and has undergone an external audit (Pilot \u2227 Audit)\n\nThus for any accepted proposal, (Rep XOR (Pilot \u2227 Audit)) holds. Mutually exclusive.\n\nSo accepted => (Rep \u2295 (Pilot \u2227 Audit)). Also exactly one condition satisfied, meaning cannot satisfy both, and must satisfy at least one.\n\nThus for accepted proposals:\n- If Rep then NOT (Pilot \u2227 Audit).\n- If Pilot\u2227Audit then NOT Rep.\n\nAlso they could be Pilot without Audit? Not allowed because condition requires Pilot AND Audit. So accepted Pilot must also be Audited. And cannot have Rep.\n\n3. No Pilot study has undergone a replication study. So Pilot -> \u00acRep. This aligns with exclusivity.\n\n4. Every externally audited proposal with non-public data is a multi-center study. So (Audit \u2227 NonPub) -> Multi.\n\n5. Every multi-center study has either public data or has undergone a replication study, or both. So Multi -> (Pub \u2228 Rep).\n\n6. There exists at least one accepted proposal with non-public data. So \u2203x (Accepted(x) \u2227 NonPub(x)).\n\n7. Every proposal that has undergone a replication study and has non-public data is externally registered. So (Rep \u2227 NonPub) -> Reg.\n\nNo other relations.\n\nGoal: Determine truth status of each statement across all possible models satisfying constraints.\n\nWe need to consider whether statements are forced (necessarily true), possibly true (some models true, some false), or impossible (always false).\n\nLet's analyze systematically.\n\nFirst, we can construct minimal models.\n\nLet\u2019s denote variables.\n\nFrom (6): there is at least one accepted proposal with NonPub. Call p0.\n\nSince it's accepted, by (2) it must satisfy exactly one of Rep or (Pilot \u2227 Audit). Since it has NonPub, we need to see which condition can hold.\n\nCase analysis:\n\nOption 1: p0 has Rep. Then by (2) it cannot have Pilot\u2227Audit. That's fine. It may or may not be Pilot (but if Pilot, would conflict with Rep due to rule 3). Actually rule 3 says Pilot -> \u00acRep, so if Rep, cannot be Pilot. So p0 is not Pilot. Also if Rep, then by (7) since NonPub, we get Reg. So p0 is Reg. Also Rep implies maybe Multi? Not directly. But from (5) Multi -> (Pub \u2228 Rep). The converse not required. So Rep doesn't force Multi.\n\nOption 2: p0 has Pilot \u2227 Audit. Then by (2) it cannot have Rep. Also Pilot implies \u00acRep consistent. So p0 is Pilot and Audited. Additionally, because it has NonPub, rule (4) says Audit \u2227 NonPub -> Multi. So p0 is Multi. Then by (5) Multi -> (Pub \u2228 Rep). Since p0 is NonPub, Pub is false, so must have Rep. But Rep contradicts earlier (cannot have Rep). Therefore this case leads to contradiction. Hence p0 cannot be Pilot\u2227Audit. Therefore the accepted NonPub proposal must have Rep (and thus be Reg). Good.\n\nThus we deduce: There exists at least one accepted proposal that has Rep and NonPub, and therefore Reg. Also not Pilot.\n\nNow evaluate each statement.\n\nA. \"There exists at least one accepted proposal that has undergone a replication study.\"\n\nFrom above, we have existence of accepted proposal with Rep (the one from (6)). So A is necessarily true (N). Because any model must contain such a proposal. Could there be alternative where the accepted NonPub proposal is Pilot\u2227Audit? We proved impossible due to rule 5. So indeed must have Rep. Therefore A is N.\n\nB. \"Every accepted proposal has undergone a replication study.\"\n\nIs it necessary? Not necessarily. Some accepted proposals could be Pilot\u2227Audit (if they have public data perhaps). Let's check possibility.\n\nConsider another accepted proposal q that is Pilot and Audited, with Public data (so Pub true). Then condition (2) satisfied via Pilot\u2227Audit, not Rep. Need to ensure consistency with other rules.\n\nCheck rule 3: Pilot -> \u00acRep, okay.\n\nRule 4: Audit \u2227 NonPub -> Multi. Here q has Pub, so NonPub false, so rule 4 irrelevant.\n\nRule 5: Multi -> (Pub \u2228 Rep). If q is not Multi, fine. If q were Multi, then need Pub or Rep; Pub true, ok.\n\nBut do we need q to be Multi? Not required. So we can have accepted Pilot Audited with Pub, not Rep, not Multi. That satisfies all constraints.\n\nThus not every accepted proposal must have Rep. So B is not necessarily true. Is it possibly false? Yes, we can have a model where there exists an accepted proposal without Rep (the Pilot one). So B is P (true in some worlds, false in others). Wait statement B asserts universal claim; we found a counterexample possible, so B is not necessarily true. It's possible false. So classification: P.\n\nC. \"Every accepted Pilot study has public data.\"\n\nWe need to examine if any accepted Pilot must have Pub. From earlier reasoning, if accepted Pilot (must be Audited per condition) and has NonPub, then rule 4 forces Multi, then rule 5 forces Pub or Rep. Since Pilot can't have Rep (rule 3), must have Pub. So if accepted Pilot has NonPub, leads to contradiction. Therefore any accepted Pilot must have Pub. So statement C is necessarily true (N). Let's verify: Suppose accepted Pilot with NonPub leads to inconsistency as shown earlier. So all accepted Pilot must have Pub. So C is N.\n\nD. \"There exists at least one externally registered proposal.\"\n\nFrom earlier, we have at least one accepted proposal with Rep and NonPub, which triggers Reg via rule 7. So there exists Reg. So D is necessarily true (N).\n\nE. \"Every externally registered proposal has been accepted.\"\n\nDo we know any Reg that might not be accepted? Rule 7 gives Reg only when Rep \u2227 NonPub. Could there be Reg from other reasons? No rule defines Reg otherwise. So any Reg must arise from Rep \u2227 NonPub. Does Rep \u2227 NonPub imply acceptance? Not directly. Acceptance requires passing ethical review and meeting condition (2). A proposal could have Rep and NonPub but not be accepted (maybe fails ethical review). Nothing prohibits that. So there could exist a Reg proposal that is not accepted. Is it possible? Let's try constructing a model where there is a proposal r with Rep, NonPub, Reg, but not accepted. Must satisfy rule 7 (which is satisfied). No other constraints on r. It could be not accepted, not Pilot, etc. So E is not necessarily true. Is it possibly false? Yes, we can have such a Reg not accepted. So statement \"Every Reg is accepted\" is false in some worlds, true in others? Could there be a world where all Reg are accepted? Possibly yes if we make no Reg outside accepted. But we need to assess necessity: Not necessary. So classification: P.\n\nF. \"There exists a proposal that is simultaneously accepted, externally audited, and has undergone a replication study.\"\n\nWe need to see if such a proposal can exist. For an accepted proposal, condition (2) says exactly one of Rep or (Pilot \u2227 Audit). So cannot have both Rep and Audit (unless Pilot also?). If it has Audit, then to be accepted it must be Pilot \u2227 Audit (since that's the second condition). But then cannot have Rep (mutually exclusive). So an accepted proposal cannot have both Rep and Audit. Therefore such a proposal cannot exist. So statement F is impossible (I).\n\nG. \"There exists a multi-center study that has not been accepted.\"\n\nCan we have a Multi that is not accepted? Yes, nothing forbids Multi being unaccepted. Need to ensure constraints: Multi -> (Pub \u2228 Rep). So we can create a proposal m that is Multi, has Pub (or Rep). Not accepted, no other constraints. So possible. Also need to avoid contradictions like Audit \u2227 NonPub -> Multi (that's fine). So we can have Multi not accepted. So statement G is possibly true (P). Not necessarily true because we could also have a world where all Multi are accepted. So classification: P.\n\nH. \"There exists an accepted Pilot study with non-public data.\"\n\nWe already argued that accepted Pilot with NonPub leads to contradiction (requires Multi, then needs Pub or Rep, but Rep prohibited, so must have Pub). Therefore impossible. So H is I.\n\nSummarize:\n\nA:N\nB:P\nC:N\nD:N\nE:P\nF:I\nG:P\nH:I\n\nReturn JSON with keys in order A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H mapping to strings \"N\",\"P\",\"I\"."
